 limiting the number of flips by picking pieces with high potential for long-term control.\n\nConsidering these factors, I\'ve narrowed it down to (A, 3) and (G, 1). After further analysis, I\'m leaning heavily towards (A, 3). It directly connects with my existing corner, solidifying my position and flipping an opponent piece that could later become a strategic anchor. It seems the most "quiet" play, but offers high control. (G, 1) does extend my territory, but after careful consideration, I\'ve chosen (A, 3) to build upon the corner I own and block the opponent\'s access.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: I currently control two corners (A1 and H1), which is a significant advantage. The game is in the mid-game phase where mobility and edge stability are crucial. I need to consolidate my position around these corners and avoid creating weaknesses (like walls on the sub-edge) that the opponent can exploit to take the remaining corners or stable edges.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(A, 3)**: This is a very strong move. It is an edge move that connects directly to my corner at A1 by flipping A2. This creates a solid block of pieces (A1-A3) on the edge. It is a "quiet" move, flipping only one piece, which helps preserve my mobility and limits the opponent\'s options.\n- **(G, 1)**: This is also a reasonable move, extending from the H1 corner. However, edge stability on the A-row seems more pressing to secure the top-left region completely.\n- **(D, 1)**: This move flips several pieces (D2-D4) on the edge. While it secures territory, flipping many pieces often opens up new moves for the opponent and creates a wall that they can exploit.\n- **(G, 6)**: This move creates a wall on the G-row (sub-edge) next to an empty H-row. This is generally a strategic error as it often allows the opponent to wedge into the edge later.\n\nReason: The move (A, 3) is the safest and most strategic choice. It consolidates the A1 corner by removing the opponent\'s piece at A2 and establishing a stable chain of pieces along the top edge. It minimizes the number of flipped pieces, thereby restricting the opponent\'s counter-play options.\n\nAnswer: (A, 3)'}
