lize that edge.\n- **(D, 8)**: A safe, quiet move on the right edge. It doesn\'t offer much attack but is stable.\n- **(H, 2)**: Flips a long diagonal, but allows the opponent to play H3 immediately, bracketing and flipping H2 back, which is a poor trade on the edge.\n- **(A, 6)**: This is a strong edge move on the top row. It flips B6 and C6 vertically, connecting to my piece at D6. Strategically, playing on A6 (an A-square) leaves the adjacent C-square (A7) empty next to the empty corner (A8). If the opponent plays A7 to attack, I can respond by taking the corner A8, securing the edge. It is a classic "creeping" move towards a corner.\n\nReason: (A, 6) provides a good balance of immediate gain (flipping two pieces) and strategic pressure. It strengthens the vertical column 6 and sets a standard trap for the A8 corner: if the opponent moves to A7, I can take A8. It does not open up immediate dangerous counter-moves for the opponent compared to other edge moves like B1 or H2.\n\nAnswer: (A, 6)'}
